1
00:00:01,100 --> 00:00:03,160
Bea is sure every one of these people thinks

2
00:00:03,180 --> 00:00:04,470
she's a disaster.

3
00:00:04,521 --> 00:00:06,741
And this week, she can hear exactly what they're

4
00:00:06,761 --> 00:00:07,301
thinking.

5
00:00:07,321 --> 00:00:09,011
Let's rewind to Monday.

6
00:00:09,709 --> 00:00:10,649
This is Bea.

7
00:00:10,669 --> 00:00:13,609
She makes the best cinnamon buns on Crumb Island.

8
00:00:13,629 --> 00:00:16,489
She also still replays something awkward she said four

9
00:00:16,509 --> 00:00:17,289
years ago.

10
00:00:17,309 --> 00:00:18,279
Most nights.

11
00:00:18,457 --> 00:00:19,637
And on Monday morning,

12
00:00:19,657 --> 00:00:22,117
Bea woke up able to hear every honest thought

13
00:00:22,137 --> 00:00:23,347
anyone had about her.

14
00:00:23,551 --> 00:00:24,571
Three rules.

15
00:00:24,591 --> 00:00:26,091
One: if someone thinks about you,

16
00:00:26,111 --> 00:00:26,891
you hear it.

17
00:00:26,911 --> 00:00:28,891
Two: it lasts seven days.

18
00:00:28,911 --> 00:00:30,171
Three: the catch.

19
00:00:30,191 --> 00:00:31,881
In very, very small print.

20
00:00:32,800 --> 00:00:33,380
Day one.

21
00:00:33,400 --> 00:00:35,290
Bea braced herself for a flood.

22
00:00:35,727 --> 00:00:36,027
MO: Ooh.

23
00:00:36,047 --> 00:00:36,897
Cinnamon.

24
00:00:37,108 --> 00:00:38,718
FINN: Those buns look incredible.

25
00:00:39,038 --> 00:00:39,658
That was it.

26
00:00:39,678 --> 00:00:40,378
All day.

27
00:00:40,398 --> 00:00:41,018
Two thoughts.

28
00:00:41,038 --> 00:00:42,328
Both about the buns.

29
00:00:42,638 --> 00:00:45,578
Psychologists call this the spotlight effect.

30
00:00:45,598 --> 00:00:49,098
In one study, students wore an embarrassing T-shirt into

31
00:00:49,118 --> 00:00:49,738
a room.

32
00:00:49,758 --> 00:00:52,298
They guessed about half the people there would remember

33
00:00:52,318 --> 00:00:52,778
it.

34
00:00:52,798 --> 00:00:54,408
Only about a quarter did.

35
00:00:55,243 --> 00:00:55,703
CUSTOMER: Wow.

36
00:00:55,723 --> 00:00:57,144
These look amazing.

37
00:00:57,164 --> 00:00:57,464
BEA: Thanks!

38
00:00:57,484 --> 00:00:58,104
You too!

39
00:00:58,124 --> 00:00:58,974
...Wait.

40
00:00:59,274 --> 00:00:59,894
Day two.

41
00:00:59,914 --> 00:01:02,404
Bea replayed that "you too" about forty times.

42
00:01:02,620 --> 00:01:04,380
BEA: He thinks you're so weird.

43
00:01:04,556 --> 00:01:06,246
And what did he actually think?

44
00:01:06,756 --> 00:01:07,296
Nothing.

45
00:01:07,316 --> 00:01:09,406
He'd forgotten before he reached the door.

46
00:01:09,716 --> 00:01:12,416
This one's called the illusion of transparency.

47
00:01:12,436 --> 00:01:14,976
We assume our nerves show on the outside.

48
00:01:14,996 --> 00:01:17,566
Usually, people see much less than we think.

49
00:01:18,317 --> 00:01:20,247
Which brings us back to day three.

50
00:01:21,267 --> 00:01:21,647
KAI: Oof.

51
00:01:21,667 --> 00:01:23,117
That happens to me all the time.

52
00:01:23,410 --> 00:01:24,300
ANA: Is she okay?

53
00:01:24,424 --> 00:01:25,314
THEO: I should help.

54
00:01:25,735 --> 00:01:29,075
Research suggests we expect people to judge our slip-ups

55
00:01:29,095 --> 00:01:31,395
more harshly than they really do.

56
00:01:31,415 --> 00:01:34,275
Partly because everyone watching has dropped a tray of

57
00:01:34,295 --> 00:01:35,145
their own.

58
00:01:35,265 --> 00:01:36,445
Theo brought a broom.

59
00:01:36,465 --> 00:01:37,805
Mo brought a sandwich.

60
00:01:37,825 --> 00:01:39,195
Nobody knows why.

61
00:01:40,220 --> 00:01:40,893
Day four.

62
00:01:40,913 --> 00:01:42,550
Bea's mum came to visit.

63
00:01:42,632 --> 00:01:44,932
Bea had always believed her mum was a little

64
00:01:44,952 --> 00:01:45,972
disappointed.

65
00:01:45,992 --> 00:01:47,012
A bakery.

66
00:01:47,032 --> 00:01:48,642
Not the life she'd hoped for.

67
00:01:48,941 --> 00:01:50,151
So Bea listened.

68
00:01:51,429 --> 00:01:52,609
MUM: I'm so proud of her.

69
00:01:52,629 --> 00:01:54,799
I've just never known how to say it.

70
00:01:56,672 --> 00:01:57,452
Day five.

71
00:01:57,472 --> 00:01:58,922
Not every thought was kind.

72
00:01:59,284 --> 00:02:01,694
CUSTOMER: The buns are a bit dry today.

73
00:02:02,109 --> 00:02:02,649
Ouch.

74
00:02:02,669 --> 00:02:03,609
And that's real.

75
00:02:03,629 --> 00:02:05,769
Some people will think critical things.

76
00:02:05,789 --> 00:02:07,289
But notice what it was about.

77
00:02:07,309 --> 00:02:08,089
The buns.

78
00:02:08,109 --> 00:02:10,119
Not Bea's worth as a person.

79
00:02:10,560 --> 00:02:11,180
Day six.

80
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:12,700
Bea did something brave.

81
00:02:12,720 --> 00:02:14,410
She told her mum she missed her.

82
00:02:14,608 --> 00:02:17,708
Showing vulnerability can feel like weakness.

83
00:02:17,728 --> 00:02:21,148
But researchers found we judge our own vulnerable moments

84
00:02:21,168 --> 00:02:23,868
far more harshly than the same moments in other

85
00:02:23,888 --> 00:02:24,738
people.

86
00:02:25,657 --> 00:02:26,437
Day seven.

87
00:02:26,457 --> 00:02:28,037
The quietest day yet.

88
00:02:28,057 --> 00:02:30,787
Except for one voice that hadn't stopped all week.

89
00:02:31,049 --> 00:02:32,229
Remember rule three?

90
00:02:32,249 --> 00:02:33,539
The very small print?

91
00:02:33,975 --> 00:02:35,075
Every thought about you.

92
00:02:35,095 --> 00:02:36,145
Including your own.

93
00:02:36,715 --> 00:02:38,165
BEA: They're laughing at you.

94
00:02:38,322 --> 00:02:39,772
BEA: You always mess things up.

95
00:02:40,062 --> 00:02:41,192
BEA: Why are you like this?

96
00:02:42,271 --> 00:02:44,971
All week, the harshest voice on Crumb Island wasn't

97
00:02:44,991 --> 00:02:45,691
a customer.

98
00:02:45,711 --> 00:02:46,761
It was Bea.

99
00:02:47,444 --> 00:02:49,104
If that voice sounds familiar,

100
00:02:49,124 --> 00:02:50,384
you're not alone.

101
00:02:50,404 --> 00:02:51,824
Next time it starts,

102
00:02:51,844 --> 00:02:54,224
try answering it the way you'd answer a friend

103
00:02:54,244 --> 00:02:55,904
who just dropped a tray.

104
00:02:55,924 --> 00:02:59,024
In experiments, people who were kind to themselves after

105
00:02:59,044 --> 00:03:01,504
a mistake were more motivated to improve,

106
00:03:01,524 --> 00:03:02,414
not less.

107
00:03:03,571 --> 00:03:04,191
Day eight.

108
00:03:04,211 --> 00:03:05,501
The thoughts were gone.

109
00:03:05,943 --> 00:03:06,403
BEA: Oops.

110
00:03:06,423 --> 00:03:07,273
...That's okay.

111
00:03:08,017 --> 00:03:10,477
Most people are busy with their own lives.

112
00:03:10,497 --> 00:03:12,077
And when they do notice you,

113
00:03:12,097 --> 00:03:14,337
they're often kinder than the voice in your head.
